WEBVTT

00:00:00.583 --> 00:00:04.500
Choose a triangle and call its angles A, B,
and C.

00:00:05.375 --> 00:00:09.000
Add the cosines of those three angles. How
large can the sum be?

00:00:09.917 --> 00:00:14.250
The answer is three halves. An equilateral
triangle reaches that value.

00:00:15.167 --> 00:00:19.333
To prove the bound for every triangle, we'll
use a theorem from Reusable,

00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:20.792
a library of Lean proofs.

00:00:21.708 --> 00:00:26.042
The theorem gives us an identity that turns
the inequality into two squares.

00:00:27.875 --> 00:00:32.583
Open the Reusable showcase. In the search
box, enter Wolstenholme defect.

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:36.833
This card is Wolstenholme's inequality, in
defect form.

00:00:37.750 --> 00:00:42.458
The Lean statement shows the exact name and
assumptions. The minimal import is the

00:00:42.458 --> 00:00:43.625
Geometry module.

00:00:44.542 --> 00:00:47.708
The provenance links you to the original
theorem in the archive.

00:00:48.625 --> 00:00:51.958
The trust row lists the axioms Lean reports
for this result.

00:00:53.792 --> 00:00:59.542
The identity assumes the three angles add to
pi. The weights x, y, and z can be any real

00:00:59.542 --> 00:01:00.083
numbers.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:04.958
Start with the sum of the squares of the
weights, then subtract the weighted cosine

00:01:04.958 --> 00:01:05.542
expression.

00:01:06.458 --> 00:01:10.375
The theorem says this difference equals the
sum of the two squares shown below.

00:01:11.167 --> 00:01:14.708
Each square is nonnegative, so the
difference is nonnegative too.

00:01:15.583 --> 00:01:19.958
That gives us the weighted inequality. The
identity does the trigonometric work.

00:01:20.875 --> 00:01:26.458
The angle sum matters. Drop that hypothesis
and set every angle to zero and every weight

00:01:26.458 --> 00:01:30.417
to one. The claimed inequality becomes six
at most three.

00:01:30.417 --> 00:01:32.083
Lean checks this counterexample.

00:01:33.917 --> 00:01:37.250
Here's the example from Recipe One of the
guide, with its import.

00:01:38.167 --> 00:01:42.250
The example takes six real numbers and the
angle sum as a hypothesis.

00:01:43.125 --> 00:01:47.875
have key applies the theorem to those inputs
and puts the equality in the local context.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:53.542
The next command passes the nonnegativity of
each square to Lean's nonlinear arithmetic

00:01:53.542 --> 00:01:54.083
tactic.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.042
Using the equality and those two facts, the
tactic closes the goal.

00:02:00.875 --> 00:02:02.792
Now use one for each of the three weights.

00:02:03.708 --> 00:02:07.958
The right side is three, and the left side
is twice the sum of the cosines.

00:02:08.875 --> 00:02:12.250
Divide both sides by two to recover our
bound of three halves.

00:02:13.167 --> 00:02:18.000
The practice file checks this specialization
in Lean. It applies the same theorem to

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.250
three ones and closes the resulting goal.

00:02:21.167 --> 00:02:26.042
That is the first reuse pattern: a general
identity becomes the particular result you

00:02:26.042 --> 00:02:26.417
need.

00:02:28.250 --> 00:02:32.667
Now imagine you need to prove that this
cubic has three distinct real roots.

00:02:32.667 --> 00:02:36.000
The graph suggests three crossings of the
horizontal axis.

00:02:36.917 --> 00:02:42.208
How could we turn that picture into evidence
Lean can use? R0011 asks for

00:02:42.208 --> 00:02:44.667
ordered sample points and alternating signs.

00:02:45.542 --> 00:02:52.625
At minus two, zero, one, and two, the exact
values are minus one, one, minus one,

00:02:52.625 --> 00:02:53.125
and three.

00:02:54.042 --> 00:02:59.250
Notice the three open intervals. Each pair
of consecutive values has strictly opposite

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.542
signs, so each interval contains a root.

00:03:02.458 --> 00:03:07.042
The intervals are disjoint. This is why
there are at least three distinct roots.

00:03:07.042 --> 00:03:09.167
We never need to compute the roots
themselves.

00:03:10.083 --> 00:03:15.250
Watch what happens if we remove the constant
term. One selected sample is now zero,

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:19.167
so this particular certificate no longer
meets the strict sign condition.

00:03:20.083 --> 00:03:24.667
Move that sample from zero to minus one. The
four signs alternate again.

00:03:24.667 --> 00:03:28.167
Different witnesses can certify a different
instance of the same problem.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.625
The practice file does something more
general. It defines a lemma named three real

00:03:34.625 --> 00:03:38.125
roots, with four ordered points and three
sign conditions.

00:03:39.042 --> 00:03:44.125
First supply the nonzero polynomial and the
vector of sample points to R zero zero one

00:03:44.125 --> 00:03:46.250
one. Two obligations remain.

00:03:47.167 --> 00:03:50.958
The first obligation is that the vector is
strictly increasing.

00:03:50.958 --> 00:03:54.875
Split into its three adjacent pairs and use
the order hypotheses.

00:03:55.792 --> 00:04:00.458
The second is that the product of each
adjacent pair of evaluations is negative.

00:04:00.458 --> 00:04:02.875
Each case is one of our sign hypotheses.

00:04:03.833 --> 00:04:08.792
We can now apply our lemma to the original
cubic. Evaluating the polynomial at zero

00:04:08.792 --> 00:04:10.167
shows it is nonzero.

00:04:11.083 --> 00:04:15.500
Then norm_num checks the three inequalities
between sample points and the three

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:18.250
inequalities between products of
evaluations.

00:04:19.167 --> 00:04:24.125
In the guide, Lean checks this chain from
the generic theorem to the particular cubic.

00:04:24.125 --> 00:04:26.250
The plot helps us choose inputs for the
proof.

00:04:28.042 --> 00:04:32.792
The next problem begins with a symmetric
real matrix. We want a certificate that its

00:04:32.792 --> 00:04:35.042
quadratic form is a sum of squares.

00:04:35.958 --> 00:04:40.542
Here is a two by two example with twos on
the diagonal and ones off the diagonal.

00:04:40.542 --> 00:04:44.958
Its characteristic polynomial is t squared
minus four t plus three.

00:04:45.875 --> 00:04:51.208
The coefficients have signs plus, minus,
plus. The determinant is three,

00:04:51.208 --> 00:04:53.417
so the two by two matrix has rank two.

00:04:54.292 --> 00:04:59.375
R0032 uses symmetry and the sign condition
on the coefficients to give

00:04:59.375 --> 00:05:00.958
positive semidefiniteness.

00:05:01.875 --> 00:05:06.333
R0033 takes positive semidefiniteness and
the rank.

00:05:06.333 --> 00:05:08.667
It gives a number of squares equal to the
rank.

00:05:09.542 --> 00:05:14.208
For this matrix we can illustrate the result
along the two diagonal directions.

00:05:14.208 --> 00:05:18.667
In ordinary coordinates, the displayed two
squares expand to the original form.

00:05:19.583 --> 00:05:25.292
Recipe Four checks the general composition
with symmetry, signs and rank as hypotheses.

00:05:25.292 --> 00:05:30.125
The companion file checks all three for this
matrix and separately proves the displayed

00:05:30.125 --> 00:05:30.667
identity.

00:05:32.500 --> 00:05:36.417
This suggests a way to build a specialized
mathematical workflow.

00:05:36.417 --> 00:05:40.458
Begin with a concrete problem and state the
guarantee you want before choosing a

00:05:40.458 --> 00:05:40.917
theorem.

00:05:41.833 --> 00:05:46.333
Suppose the requested output is a
certificate that the quadratic form is a sum

00:05:46.333 --> 00:05:50.583
of exactly k squares. Find a theorem whose
conclusion has that shape.

00:05:51.500 --> 00:05:56.167
Read the argument slots. Positive
semidefiniteness can come from the preceding

00:05:56.167 --> 00:06:00.667
theorem. Symmetry, coefficient signs, and
rank still need evidence.

00:06:01.625 --> 00:06:07.042
A calculation with exact coefficients can
propose witnesses. The proof needs the exact

00:06:07.042 --> 00:06:09.375
facts accepted at the input of each theorem.

00:06:10.292 --> 00:06:15.333
The guide already contains the composed Lean
term. A successful check of the abstract

00:06:15.333 --> 00:06:18.583
theorem confirms that the pieces fit under
its hypotheses.

00:06:19.458 --> 00:06:24.000
To obtain a certificate for a specific
matrix, you also need Lean proofs of those

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:25.792
hypotheses for that matrix.

00:06:26.708 --> 00:06:30.917
Excovery already has a planner for tools
with typed inputs and outputs.

00:06:30.917 --> 00:06:35.250
This view sketches a future interface for
working at the level of theorem cards.

00:06:36.167 --> 00:06:42.083
It connects a problem, candidate theorems,
open obligations, and the final Lean check.

00:06:42.083 --> 00:06:44.500
The guide already gives us examples to build
from.

00:06:46.333 --> 00:06:50.792
Pick the next case you would like to prove.
In the triangle, change the weights.

00:06:50.792 --> 00:06:53.125
In the cubic, change the sample points.

00:06:54.042 --> 00:06:59.083
For the matrix, start from the conclusion
and work back to the exact coefficients and

00:06:59.083 --> 00:07:00.167
rank you can certify.

00:07:01.042 --> 00:07:06.042
A further design uses signs and a degree
bound to certify all roots are real,

00:07:06.042 --> 00:07:09.792
then monicity and a valid index to apply
Newton's inequalities.

00:07:10.708 --> 00:07:15.125
Use the guide and the theorem cards to
follow each step to its Lean statement.

00:07:15.125 --> 00:07:18.375
The downloadable Lean files give you a
working starting point.
